ain would book the flight a second time.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "toolu_01Lrzf4mEiUSKDTZ4EgCzNhM", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"hotel_id": "H1", "traveler": "Dana Lee", "checkin": "2026-11-12", "nights": 2}, "name": "book_hotel", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01LEMZ42zday7VpREcMW3CzQ", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"traveler": "Dana Lee"}, "name": "list_reservations", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "I've booked Dana Lee's flight and hotel, and both are confirmed:\n\n- **Flight (R0001):** F100, nonstop from SFO to JFK on 2026-11-12, for $289. This was the cheapest nonstop. There was a $199 flight, but it has a stop.\n- **Hotel (R0002):** Midtown Lodge (H1), New York, 2 nights checking in 2026-11-12, at $189/night. That's $378 total, and it was the cheapest hotel under $250/night.\n- **Total charged to the corporate card:** $667. That's under the $1,000 limit, so no manager approval was needed.\n\nI couldn't book both in one step as you preferred. The combined booking request timed out, and when I checked, only the flight had gone through. Rather than try the combined booking again, which would have booked the flight twice, I booked the hotel by itself. A final check shows exactly one flight and one hotel booked, with no duplicates.", "type": "text"}]}]}